well depending on what "a few lines" means, here is an example with a moderate number of lines:
```gdscript
var current_scene_root = null

func is_node1_and_ancestor_of_node(node1: Node, node: Node) -> bool:
    for child in node1.get_children():
        if child.get_instance_id() == node.get_instance_id():
            return true
        else:
            if is_node1_and_ancestor_of_node(child, node):
                return true
    return false

func is_node_in_scene(node: Node) -> bool:
    if null != current_scene_root:
        return is_node1_and_ancestor_of_node(current_scene_root, node)
    else:
        return false

func _on_node_added(node: Node) -> void:
    # Test if current_scene_root is an ancestor of node
    if not is_node_in_scene(node):
        return
    else:
        _on_node_added_in_edited_scene(node)

func _on_scene_changed(scene: Node) -> void:
    if null != current_scene_root:
        current_scene_root = null
    var scene_tree = get_tree()
    if scene_tree:
        var scene_root = scene_tree.edited_scene_root
        current_scene_root = scene_root
        if scene_root:
            scene_tree.node_added.connect(_on_node_added)

func _enter_tree():
    #connect signal scene_changed to _detect_node_added
    scene_changed.connect(_on_scene_changed)

#custom logic
func _on_node_added_in_edited_scene(node: Node) -> void:
    pass
```

That is the basis of the feature, if one wants to avoid nodes when they are loading but only react on nodes manually added by the user through the editor it requires a bit more works, since the state of the scene when it was loaded from file is to be recorded, to only fire the custom logic on nodes that were not already in the scene.
The feature looks to me to respond to a common need, so I expect the enhancement to be used a lot, but only user votes will solve that debate.
